00:50:29.620 --> 00:50:30.943
But I was like,

00:50:30.943 --> 00:50:32.430
"Well if Rhett's gonna go, I'll go."

00:50:32.430 --> 00:50:34.000
I still may hate it and be the one

00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:35.970
that means we're not gonna go again.

00:50:35.970 --> 00:50:40.107
But I, you know we're
hatching these ideas of like,

00:50:40.107 --> 00:50:41.657
"Okay we can make.

00:50:41.657 --> 00:50:43.927
"We make videos for presentations.

00:50:43.927 --> 00:50:47.480
"We are funny, we're a duo."

00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:50.030
Like that was our biggest
identity in high school

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:52.476
above these other things
we're talking about.

00:50:52.476 --> 00:50:55.901
So for me, without knowing
the application of it

00:50:55.901 --> 00:50:58.000
I was just like, this is.

00:50:58.000 --> 00:50:59.760
I'm comfortable in this friendship.

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:04.340
I have, I have hopes.

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:07.040
I don't know that I had my own dreams.

00:51:07.040 --> 00:51:08.720
I had my own hopes.

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:12.140
So I kind of felt like
when you brought the dream

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:13.183
and attached it to,

00:51:13.183 --> 00:51:15.880
like my hope immediately paid off.

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:18.080
So it was like this huge relief.

00:51:18.080 --> 00:51:21.550
I felt like as much as
you practice basketball

00:51:21.550 --> 00:51:25.086
with your special shoes
and your blocks and like,

00:51:25.086 --> 00:51:28.930
we couldn't go and swim in the river

00:51:28.930 --> 00:51:32.430
until you had shot 100 basketball shots.

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:33.680
Maybe 300.

00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:35.922
Like I would go over to your house

00:51:35.922 --> 00:51:37.323
and just sit in your
yard and just be like,

00:51:37.323 --> 00:51:38.440
"What the hell man?"

00:51:38.440 --> 00:51:40.400
Yeah.
It's like hurry up dude.

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:44.300
It was just like, I was just
waiting on you to get done

00:51:44.300 --> 00:51:46.690
so we could go chase cows.

00:51:46.690 --> 00:51:51.690
So that dynamic was a play on
the macro scale too of like,

00:51:52.006 --> 00:51:57.006
who am I to say, hey yeah
let's, let's hatch a plan

00:51:57.850 --> 00:51:59.690
to be filmmakers.

00:51:59.690 --> 00:52:04.350
You know, I've felt like I
was at the mercy of like,

00:52:04.350 --> 00:52:06.630
this basketball plan.

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:09.910
So when you start saying things like,

00:52:09.910 --> 00:52:11.390
we can do this.

00:52:11.390 --> 00:52:14.230
We can do, I can do both
so that we can do this

00:52:15.358 --> 00:52:16.790
and if I, because that's
what I really want to do.

00:52:16.790 --> 00:52:18.080
We need to do something.

00:52:18.080 --> 00:52:22.020
It's like you know,
making a blood oath for me

00:52:22.020 --> 00:52:26.200
was such a validation of
my hopes and it was like,

00:52:26.200 --> 00:52:28.590
oh this is official.

00:52:28.590 --> 00:52:33.560
Rhett actually prioritizes
what we can do together

00:52:33.560 --> 00:52:34.730
over this basketball thing.

00:52:34.730 --> 00:52:39.120
Because you know, leading
up to that for years

00:52:39.120 --> 00:52:41.930
it wasn't like, we didn't have
those type of conversations

00:52:41.930 --> 00:52:44.032
about everything you know?

00:52:44.032 --> 00:52:46.330
It wasn't, I'm sure there were many times

00:52:46.330 --> 00:52:49.084
where there were like question
marks and it was like,

00:52:49.084 --> 00:52:50.090
well I'm not gonna push this.

00:52:50.090 --> 00:52:55.090
I'm also not one to like have
specific futuristic dreams.

00:52:55.270 --> 00:52:59.450
So once you made it official with the oath

00:52:59.450 --> 00:53:01.780
and then once you start
making these plans.

00:53:01.780 --> 00:53:03.907
I'm like, yes, yes, yes.

00:53:03.907 --> 00:53:06.610
It was like a relief to me.

00:53:06.610 --> 00:53:08.420
Because it's just
something that I was hoping

00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:10.670
that would come together
but I couldn't push.

00:53:11.532 --> 00:53:15.601
And then when you come
back and you're like,

00:53:15.601 --> 00:53:19.587
you know, once you realize
that's not gonna happen you know?

00:53:19.587 --> 00:53:23.194
And the reason that, and
you know what happened.

00:53:23.194 --> 00:53:27.540
We got very serious about
the band that summer.

00:53:27.540 --> 00:53:30.450
That was when.
After junior year.

00:53:30.450 --> 00:53:32.690
Yeah, so that was when you know,

00:53:32.690 --> 00:53:34.510
towards the second half of junior year

00:53:34.510 --> 00:53:37.420
and the summer before senior year is when

00:53:37.420 --> 00:53:40.000
you're kind of in that timeframe

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:44.923
is when our music went from
singing like country covers,

00:53:46.130 --> 00:53:47.070
two lead singers

00:53:47.070 --> 00:53:50.310
with cowboy hats and white
t-shirts and jeans to like,

00:53:50.310 --> 00:53:51.809
I'm gonna actually.

00:53:51.809 --> 00:53:53.990
I'm gonna get a guitar, I'm
gonna learn how to play guitar

00:53:53.990 --> 00:53:55.290
and write music.

00:53:55.290 --> 00:53:57.500
Because I feel like we need to take this

00:53:57.500 --> 00:53:58.650
in a certain direction.

00:53:58.650 --> 00:54:00.670
We don't need to be playing
other people's songs.

00:54:00.670 --> 00:54:02.583
We need to be writing our own music.

00:54:02.583 --> 00:54:03.533
Yeah.

00:54:04.916 --> 00:54:07.930
And I mean I always tell that story,

00:54:07.930 --> 00:54:10.490
like I was embarrassed that
we had two lead singers

00:54:10.490 --> 00:54:12.410
and so I started playing guitar.

00:54:12.410 --> 00:54:13.690
That's the joke version.

00:54:13.690 --> 00:54:15.750
The real version is is I was like,

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:17.937
we need to write our own music.

00:54:17.937 --> 00:54:19.660
You know listen, if we're gonna be a band

00:54:19.660 --> 00:54:22.120
we need to be defining some kind of style

00:54:22.120 --> 00:54:23.626
or something right?

00:54:23.626 --> 00:54:24.600
And it doesn't need to be The Eagles.

00:54:24.600 --> 00:54:26.070
Right and so.

00:54:26.070 --> 00:54:28.340
Which is what Benny
had written in the 70s.

00:54:28.340 --> 00:54:32.410
And again I am, if I'm
really good at anything

00:54:32.410 --> 00:54:34.870
it's drinking my own Kool-Aid right?

00:54:34.870 --> 00:54:38.860
It's like I am great at making
and drinking my own Kool-Aid.

00:54:38.860 --> 00:54:40.880
That's kind of what propels me.

00:54:40.880 --> 00:54:43.650
I think I like it too apparently.

00:54:43.650 --> 00:54:45.880
My gasoline is my Kool-Aid

00:54:45.880 --> 00:54:49.720
and so once we start writing music,

00:54:49.720 --> 00:54:52.890
by the time basketball
season rolls around,

00:54:52.890 --> 00:54:55.397
I specifically remember thinking,

00:54:55.397 --> 00:54:57.657
"Okay I'm gonna do this because you know,

00:54:57.657 --> 00:54:59.267
"I have gotten to this point

00:54:59.267 --> 00:55:01.910
"and I've done all this
work for basketball."

00:55:01.910 --> 00:55:05.233
I'm gonna try to some extent but,

00:55:06.150 --> 00:55:07.960
I'm not gonna play college basketball.

00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:09.080
I had made a decision

00:55:09.080 --> 00:55:11.688
before I even started
playing my senior year

00:55:11.688 --> 00:55:12.521
that I wasn't gonna
play college basketball.

00:55:12.521 --> 00:55:14.897
I wasn't telling my dad
